@prefix rdf:    <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:   <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:    <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:    <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix prov:   <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix safety: <http://internal/sustainment/safety#> .

<http://internal/sustainment/safety/matrix> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Safety risk matrix and acceptance ladder — SEED policy (pending domain-owner ratification)" ;
    rdfs:comment """The severity x probability -> risk level table, and the risk level -> acceptance
authority ladder. POLICY AS DATA, on pcn_disposition_rules.ttl's exact precedent: the MECHANISM
(resolve a hazard's severity and probability to a level, resolve the level to the authority the
acceptance requires, refuse loudly on an unrecognized value) lives in the engine; the ROWS live
here, versioned and reproducible through the manifest partition like every other standards
artifact, so the drift-check covers policy too. Change the programme's matrix -> edit this file ->
re-ingest -> the drafter follows source, no code deploy.

THIS IS THE LOAD-BEARING DECISION OF ADR-0051 (§2). The matrix and the ladder are exactly what
differs between programmes. In code, the second customer is a fork of the drafting engine; as
ratifiable data, the second customer is an overlay file and a re-prime.

THE MATRIX CITES ITS SOURCE AS OF 2026-09-12 AND IS NO LONGER A READING. v1 was "the agent's
reading of MIL-STD-882 convention" with prov:wasDerivedFrom deliberately empty. It was then
TRANSCRIBED from the standard, and FIVE OF TWENTY CELLS WERE WRONG — all five too permissive.
So the emptiness was honest and the content was not, which is the pairing worth remembering: an
honest label on a wrong table still routes a real acceptance to the wrong authority.

**THE TWO HALVES OF THIS FILE HAVE DIFFERENT PROVENANCE AND MUST NOT SHARE A CITATION.**

  THE MATRIX (severity x probability -> risk level)
      MIL-STD-882E, Table III "Risk assessment matrix", document page 12, in the 4.3.3
      risk-assessment text. VERIFIED against the standard's own PDF, cell by cell.
      882E 4.3.3.d: "The definitions in Tables I and II, and the RACs in Table III shall be
      used, unless tailored alternative definitions and/or a tailored matrix are formally
      approved in accordance with DoD Component policy."

  THE ACCEPTANCE LADDER (risk level -> who may accept)
      NOT IN 882E. 4.3.7, read verbatim from the standard: "the risks shall be accepted by
      the appropriate authority as defined in DoDI 5000.02" — it defers, and names nobody.

      READ FROM SOURCE 2026-09-14, and the ladder is now the standard's rather than a fixture.
      DoDI 5000.88 PARA 3.6.e.(1)(b)1, Directives Division PDF, November 18 2020 issue:

          High         -> the Component Acquisition Executive (CAE), or the Defense
                          Acquisition Executive (DAE)
          Serious      -> program executive officer-LEVEL
          Medium, Low  -> the Program Manager

      THAT IS THE SEEDED LADDER, CONFIRMED CELL FOR CELL. The 2026-09-12 corroboration — a
      search summary plus a secondary page, with esd.whs.mil returning HTTP 403 — has been
      replaced by the document. The ladder and the matrix are now EQUALLY SOURCED, which is
      the state the previous paragraph said must be reached before a real acceptance routes.

      AND THE SAME PARAGRAPH CARRIES THE CONCURRENCE RULE, which matters because it means the
      two-act sequence is required by the INSTRUCTION as well as by the standard: the user
      representative, as defined in MIL-STD-882E, is part of this process throughout the
      life-cycle and provides formal concurrence BEFORE serious and high risk acceptance
      decisions. ADR-0051 §5.1 built that from 882E 4.3.7 alone; it now has two sources that
      do not depend on each other, and the seal over it does not change.

  TWO NUANCES THAT ARE OVERLAY-TAILORABLE, NOT SEED — carried because collapsing either one
  into the seed would state something narrower than the instruction does.

      "CAE (or DAE)". The High authority may be the DEFENSE Acquisition Executive rather than
      the Component one. The seed names a single audience per level because the audience key
      convention has one; WHICH office fills it is a programme fact, and an overlay row that
      points `risk_acceptance_high:SUSTAINMENT` at a DAE-held group is not a departure from
      the standard and needs no SSPP ratifier.

      "Program executive officer-LEVEL" is a LEVEL, not necessarily the PEO in person. A
      deployment that grants Serious acceptance to an officer at that level who is not the PEO
      is inside the instruction. Reading the word as the post would make the seed stricter than
      its source — and a ladder that is wrong in the RESTRICTIVE direction fails differently
      from a permissive one (it blocks a lawful acceptance rather than admitting an unlawful
      signature) but it is still wrong, and it is the direction nobody files a defect about.

      FOR JOINT PROGRAMS the acceptance authorities reside within the LEAD DoD COMPONENT. That
      is an overlay fact and not a platform one: nothing in this file or in task_grants.yaml
      should encode which component leads, because the answer differs per programme and a seed
      that guessed would be an entitlement decision made by a manifest field.

WHAT A PROGRAMME RATIFIES IS ITS TAILORING, NOT THE STANDARD. A cell that matches Table III needs
no local ratifier; it has one already. An OVERLAY row that departs from Table III does, and the
ratifier is the signatory of that programme's System Safety Program Plan. That is the only place
a named human is required, and it is the programme's person rather than ours.

KEEP THIS A FLAT TABLE. Never a rule language: nesting, expressions and priorities are
code-as-policy's revenge, and the neighbouring disposition rules file says so from experience.""" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://cdn.ymaws.com/system-safety.org/resource/resmgr/documents/MIL-STD-882E.pdf> ,
                        <https://www.esd.whs.mil/Portals/54/Documents/DD/issuances/dodi/500088p.PDF> ;
    safety:derivedFromStandard "MIL-STD-882E, Table III (Risk assessment matrix), document page 12, section 4.3.3" ;
    # THE LADDER'S OWN CITATION, SEPARATE FROM THE MATRIX'S BY DESIGN. The two were kept apart
    # while the ladder was attributed and they stay apart now that it is read: they are two
    # documents, and one predicate covering both would make a later change to either invisible.
    safety:ladderDerivedFromInstruction "DoDI 5000.88, PARA 3.6.e.(1)(b)1, November 18 2020 issue — CAE (or DAE) for High, program executive officer-level for Serious, PM for Medium and Low; and the user representative provides formal concurrence before all Serious and High acceptance decisions." ;
    safety:ratificationStatus "matrix: TRANSCRIBED AND VERIFIED against MIL-STD-882E Table III 2026-09-12, no local ratifier required for cells matching the standard. Acceptance ladder: READ FROM SOURCE 2026-09-14 — DoDI 5000.88 PARA 3.6.e.(1)(b)1 — and it MATCHES the seed; no local ratifier required. Tailoring: an overlay row departing from Table III or from the instruction's ladder requires the programme SSPP signatory; naming WHICH office holds a level (CAE vs DAE, PEO-level, lead Component on a joint programme) is not a departure and needs no ratifier." .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SEVERITY — the closed vocabulary. A value outside it is a REFUSAL at draft
# time, naming this file, never a coerced default (the qualification-status
# vocabulary's rule, applied here).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
safety:Catastrophic a safety:SeverityCategory ;
    rdfs:label "Catastrophic" ; safety:severityCode "I"   ; safety:severityRank 1 ;
    rdfs:comment "Could result in death, permanent total disability, or irreversible environmental damage." .
safety:Critical a safety:SeverityCategory ;
    rdfs:label "Critical" ; safety:severityCode "II"  ; safety:severityRank 2 ;
    rdfs:comment "Could result in permanent partial disability or severe injury." .
safety:Marginal a safety:SeverityCategory ;
    rdfs:label "Marginal" ; safety:severityCode "III" ; safety:severityRank 3 ;
    rdfs:comment "Could result in injury or occupational illness resulting in lost workdays." .
safety:Negligible a safety:SeverityCategory ;
    rdfs:label "Negligible" ; safety:severityCode "IV" ; safety:severityRank 4 ;
    rdfs:comment "Could result in injury or illness not resulting in lost workdays." .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PROBABILITY — likewise closed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
safety:Frequent a safety:ProbabilityLevel ;
    rdfs:label "Frequent" ; safety:probabilityCode "A" ; safety:probabilityRank 1 .
safety:Probable a safety:ProbabilityLevel ;
    rdfs:label "Probable" ; safety:probabilityCode "B" ; safety:probabilityRank 2 .
safety:Occasional a safety:ProbabilityLevel ;
    rdfs:label "Occasional" ; safety:probabilityCode "C" ; safety:probabilityRank 3 .
safety:Remote a safety:ProbabilityLevel ;
    rdfs:label "Remote" ; safety:probabilityCode "D" ; safety:probabilityRank 4 .
safety:Improbable a safety:ProbabilityLevel ;
    rdfs:label "Improbable" ; safety:probabilityCode "E" ; safety:probabilityRank 5 .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RISK LEVELS, and the AUTHORITY each one's acceptance requires.
#
# The audience key is what policy/task_grants.yaml grants against, and it is
# recorded HERE rather than in code because the ladder is the half a programme
# re-negotiates. ADR-0051 §5: one audience per level, because an authority
# level IS a group and `grant_to` cannot name one yet.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `safety:acceptanceAuthorityTier` ADDED 2026-09-14, and it is added because the ladder
# could not be SEALED without it. The audience key says which queue the acceptance lands
# in; it does not say which OFFICE the instruction requires, so a seal over these rows
# could only ever have asserted that four keys were spelled consistently with themselves —
# the fixture-that-cannot-fail, one field short of the fact under test.
#
# The tier is the half DoDI 5000.88 PARA 3.6.e.(1)(b)1 actually names, so it is the half a
# transcription seal must compare. `safety:authorityRank` orders the offices for the
# DIRECTIONAL assertion (1 most senior), exactly as `safety:riskRank` orders the levels:
# the failure nobody files is a ladder naming a MORE JUNIOR office than the instruction,
# because the queue works, the card renders, and the wrong person signs.
safety:High a safety:RiskLevel ;
    rdfs:label "High" ; safety:riskRank 1 ;
    safety:acceptanceAuthorityTier "CAE_OR_DAE" ; safety:authorityRank 1 ;
    safety:acceptanceAudience "risk_acceptance_high:SUSTAINMENT" .
safety:Serious a safety:RiskLevel ;
    rdfs:label "Serious" ; safety:riskRank 2 ;
    safety:acceptanceAuthorityTier "PEO_LEVEL" ; safety:authorityRank 2 ;
    safety:acceptanceAudience "risk_acceptance_serious:SUSTAINMENT" .
safety:Medium a safety:RiskLevel ;
    rdfs:label "Medium" ; safety:riskRank 3 ;
    safety:acceptanceAuthorityTier "PM" ; safety:authorityRank 3 ;
    safety:acceptanceAudience "risk_acceptance_medium:SUSTAINMENT" .
safety:Low a safety:RiskLevel ;
    rdfs:label "Low" ; safety:riskRank 4 ;
    safety:acceptanceAuthorityTier "PM" ; safety:authorityRank 3 ;
    safety:acceptanceAudience "risk_acceptance_low:SUSTAINMENT" .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE MATRIX — TRANSCRIBED FROM MIL-STD-882E TABLE III, NOT REMEMBERED.
#
# CORRECTED 2026-09-12 AGAINST THE STANDARD, AND FIVE OF TWENTY CELLS WERE WRONG.
# v1 was labelled "the agent's reading of MIL-STD-882 convention", which was an
# honest label for something written from memory — and memory got a quarter of the
# table wrong. The five: II/E was Low (is Medium), III/B was Medium (is Serious),
# III/D was Low (is Medium), III/E was Low (is Medium), IV/B was Low (is Medium).
# EVERY ERROR WAS IN THE SAME DIRECTION — too permissive. A remembered matrix does
# not fail randomly; it drifts toward "that sounds about right", and in this domain
# that direction understates risk and routes the acceptance to a junior authority.
#
# All twenty are present: a missing cell must be a REFUSAL a reader can see, not a
# gap the drafter fills by interpolating between neighbours.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
[] a safety:MatrixCell ; safety:whenSeverity "I"   ; safety:whenProbability "A" ; safety:yieldsRiskLevel safety:High .
[] a safety:MatrixCell ; safety:whenSeverity "I"   ; safety:whenProbability "B" ; safety:yieldsRiskLevel safety:High .
[] a safety:MatrixCell ; safety:whenSeverity "I"   ; safety:whenProbability "C" ; safety:yieldsRiskLevel safety:High .
[] a safety:MatrixCell ; safety:whenSeverity "I"   ; safety:whenProbability "D" ; safety:yieldsRiskLevel safety:Serious .
[] a safety:MatrixCell ; safety:whenSeverity "I"   ; safety:whenProbability "E" ; safety:yieldsRiskLevel safety:Medium .

[] a safety:MatrixCell ; safety:whenSeverity "II"  ; safety:whenProbability "A" ; safety:yieldsRiskLevel safety:High .
[] a safety:MatrixCell ; safety:whenSeverity "II"  ; safety:whenProbability "B" ; safety:yieldsRiskLevel safety:High .
[] a safety:MatrixCell ; safety:whenSeverity "II"  ; safety:whenProbability "C" ; safety:yieldsRiskLevel safety:Serious .
[] a safety:MatrixCell ; safety:whenSeverity "II"  ; safety:whenProbability "D" ; safety:yieldsRiskLevel safety:Medium .
[] a safety:MatrixCell ; safety:whenSeverity "II"  ; safety:whenProbability "E" ; safety:yieldsRiskLevel safety:Medium .

[] a safety:MatrixCell ; safety:whenSeverity "III" ; safety:whenProbability "A" ; safety:yieldsRiskLevel safety:Serious .
[] a safety:MatrixCell ; safety:whenSeverity "III" ; safety:whenProbability "B" ; safety:yieldsRiskLevel safety:Serious .
[] a safety:MatrixCell ; safety:whenSeverity "III" ; safety:whenProbability "C" ; safety:yieldsRiskLevel safety:Medium .
[] a safety:MatrixCell ; safety:whenSeverity "III" ; safety:whenProbability "D" ; safety:yieldsRiskLevel safety:Medium .
[] a safety:MatrixCell ; safety:whenSeverity "III" ; safety:whenProbability "E" ; safety:yieldsRiskLevel safety:Medium .

[] a safety:MatrixCell ; safety:whenSeverity "IV"  ; safety:whenProbability "A" ; safety:yieldsRiskLevel safety:Medium .
[] a safety:MatrixCell ; safety:whenSeverity "IV"  ; safety:whenProbability "B" ; safety:yieldsRiskLevel safety:Medium .
[] a safety:MatrixCell ; safety:whenSeverity "IV"  ; safety:whenProbability "C" ; safety:yieldsRiskLevel safety:Low .
[] a safety:MatrixCell ; safety:whenSeverity "IV"  ; safety:whenProbability "D" ; safety:yieldsRiskLevel safety:Low .
[] a safety:MatrixCell ; safety:whenSeverity "IV"  ; safety:whenProbability "E" ; safety:yieldsRiskLevel safety:Low .

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PROBABILITY LEVEL F (ELIMINATED) IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, AND THAT IS STATED
# RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE NOTICED.
#
# Table III has a SIXTH probability row — Eliminated (F) — whose entire row reads
# "Eliminated" rather than a risk level. It is not a risk band; it is the state a
# hazard reaches when it no longer exists, and 4.3.4's goal is "always to eliminate
# the hazard if possible."
#
# It is omitted here because an eliminated hazard is not ASSESSED, and this file is
# the assessment table. In this vocabulary that state is `safety:hazardStatus`
# `closed`, not a probability. Recorded so a later reader can tell a considered
# omission from a transcription that stopped at E — the same distinction §1's
# survey header draws between "surveyed and absent" and "never surveyed".
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# I/E — CORRECTED FRAMING 2026-09-12. It was flagged here as "the row most likely
# to be wrong". It is not wrong: Medium IS Table III's own value for
# Catastrophic/Improbable.
#
# What it is, is THE ROW MOST COMMONLY TAILORED UPWARD. Some programmes hold that a
# catastrophic outcome never falls below Serious regardless of probability, because
# the acceptance decision should reach a senior authority whatever the odds. 882E
# 4.3.3.d permits exactly that — "unless tailored alternative definitions and/or a
# tailored matrix are formally approved in accordance with DoD Component policy",
# with alternates derived from Tables I through III.
#
# So this is the row a programme changes first, it changes it in an OVERLAY rather
# than here, and changing it moves the audience the acceptance routes to — which is
# the whole point of the ladder being data. A tailored cell is the one place a named
# ratifier is genuinely required, because it departs from the standard.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
